+\section{Reflected kernel transport and every power of the norm kernel}
+
+Write $I_2=\ker(\A_2\to\A_\chi)$ and $I_N=\ker\pi_N$.
+The coefficient parametrizations in (RF.10) and (RF.11) prove
+\[
+ \operatorname{Ann}_{\C[S]}I_2=(\chi),\qquad
+ \operatorname{Ann}_{\C[S]}I_N=(\chi^\dagger).
+ \tag{RF.18}
+\]
+Indeed a polynomial $p$ annihilates the cyclic generator $[\chi]$
+of the second kernel precisely when $\chi\chi^\dagger\mid p\chi$,
+equivalently $\chi^\dagger\mid p$; the first assertion follows
+with $\chi^2$ in place of $\chi\chi^\dagger$. Thus the original
+$\C[S]$-action on $I_N$ descends to $\A_\chi$ exactly when
+$\chi^\dagger\mid\chi$, or equivalently $\chi^\dagger=\chi$.
+The original action always descends to $\A_{\chi^\dagger}$.
+
+There is nevertheless a direct reflected identification of both
+kernel spaces, retaining the exact scalar action:
+\[
+ F:I_2\xrightarrow{\sim}I_N,\quad
+ F([\chi f]_{\chi^2})=[\chi f^\star]_{N_\chi},\qquad
+ G([\chi h]_{N_\chi})=[\chi h^\star]_{\chi^2}.
+ \tag{RF.19}
+\]
+If $\chi\mid f_1-f_2$, then $\chi^\dagger\mid(f_1-f_2)^\star$
+by (RF.1), proving that $F$ is well-defined. If
+$\chi^\dagger\mid h_1-h_2$, then
+$\chi\mid(h_1-h_2)^\star$, proving the assertion for $G$.
+The identity $\star^2=1$ proves $GF=1$ and $FG=1$.
+Both maps preserve addition and conjugate complex scalars.
+For every $p\in\C[S]$ their complete intertwining law is
+\[
+ F(p(S)x)=p^\star(S)F(x),\qquad
+ FM_S=(kI-M_S)F.
+ \tag{RF.20}
+\]
+This follows by applying $\star$ to the product $pf$. The
+labelled lift sends $\tau$ to $\tau$ and $(\lambda_0,x)$ to
+$(\lambda_0,Fx)$; its scalar law is
+$F(\alpha x)=\overline\alpha F(x)$. It is therefore a
+conjugate-linear fibre map with the original support retained.
+
+The exact product on the coefficient module of $I_N$ is
+\[
+ [f]\diamond[h]=[\chi fh]_{\chi^\dagger},\qquad
+ \iota_N([f]\diamond[h])=\iota_N[f]\,\iota_N[h].
+ \tag{RF.21}
+\]
+Changing $f$ or $h$ by a multiple of $\chi^\dagger$ changes the
+right-hand representative by a multiple of that polynomial.
+The second equality is the identity $\chi(\chi fh)=\chi^2fh$
+modulo $N_\chi$. This proves the full product transport; no
+algebra-isomorphism assertion is imposed on the semilinear map $F$.
+
+\begin{theorem}
+For every integer $t\ge1$, retaining all root orders from (RF.6),
+\[
+ \dim_{\C} I_N^t
+    =\sum_{\lambda:r_\lambda>0}
+       \max\{b_\lambda-(t-1)a_\lambda,0\}.
+ \tag{RF.22}
+\]
+The kernel is nilpotent exactly when every reflected root occurs
+among the original roots. In this case its exact nilpotence index is
+\[
+ \max_{\lambda:b_\lambda>0}
+       \left\lceil\frac{a_\lambda+b_\lambda}{a_\lambda}\right\rceil.
+ \tag{RF.23}
+\]
+In general its persistent part is the algebra
+\[
+ I_N^\infty:=\bigcap_{t\ge1}I_N^t
+   \simeq\prod_{\lambda:a_\lambda=0,\ b_\lambda>0}
+           \C[S]/((S-\lambda)^{b_\lambda}).
+ \tag{RF.24}
+\]
+The isomorphism is the restriction of the original CRT residue map.
+An empty product here denotes the zero algebra. Its unit when
+nonzero is the idempotent with value one in those reflected-only
+blocks and zero in all other blocks. Projection to $\A_\chi$
+sends it to zero, and its labelled image is supported zero.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+In the block at $\lambda$ put $z=S-\lambda$ and write
+$\chi=z^{a_\lambda}U_\lambda$ with $U_\lambda(\lambda)\ne0$.
+The original polynomial $U_\lambda$ remains part of the
+parametrization. It is invertible in
+$\C[S]/(z^{a_\lambda+b_\lambda})$: its constant coefficient
+is nonzero, and the finite geometric expansion in its nilpotent
+nonconstant part gives an inverse. Therefore the ideal generated
+by $\chi^t$ in this block is exactly $(z^{t a_\lambda})$.
+Its basis is $z^{t a_\lambda},\ldots,
+z^{a_\lambda+b_\lambda-1}$ when the lower index is smaller
+than the upper length, and is empty otherwise. This proves
+(RF.22), including $a_\lambda=0$.
+
+If $a_\lambda=0<b_\lambda$, that ideal is the whole nonzero
+block for every $t$, so nilpotence is impossible. Otherwise
+each block with $b_\lambda>0$ vanishes precisely when
+$t a_\lambda\ge a_\lambda+b_\lambda$. Taking the largest
+of these exact integer thresholds proves (RF.23). The maximum
+is over a nonempty set because $\deg\chi^\dagger=q\ge1$.
+There are finitely many blocks. The blocks with $a_\lambda>0$
+all vanish after a common finite threshold, whereas the others
+persist unchanged. The CRT isomorphism and its inverse from
+Bezout consequently give (RF.24) and the stated idempotent.
+Reduction modulo $\chi$ omits precisely the blocks where
+$a_\lambda=0$, proving its amplitude image and the assertion
+about its support-preserving lift.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a complete overlapping example keep $k=2$, $c=1$ and
+put $A=S-(2+i)$, $B=S-i$, so $A=B-2$. For
+$\chi=A^2B$ the retained polynomials are
+\[
+ \chi^\dagger=AB^2,\quad g=AB,\quad
+ N_\chi=A^3B^3,\quad d=A^3B^2,\quad\ell=A^4B^3.
+ \tag{RF.25}
+\]
+In particular
+$\chi^2-N_\chi=A^3B^2(A-B)=-2A^3B^2$ has degree five,
+so the square of $[\chi]$ in the degree-six norm algebra is
+nonzero. Its cube is zero because $N_\chi\mid A^6B^3$.
+Formula (RF.22) gives dimensions $3,1,0$ for the first three
+powers, with every original root order retained. If representatives
+$P,Q$ satisfy $P-Q=dR$, their common refinement is explicitly
+\[
+ T=P+\tfrac12R\chi^2=Q+\tfrac12RN_\chi,
+ \tag{RF.26}
+\]
+since $N_\chi-\chi^2=2d$. Its residues are $P$ modulo
+$\chi^2$ and $Q$ modulo $N_\chi$. Thus the full mixed
+fibre-product map is available even though the norm kernel and
+the square-zero conormal kernel have different products.
+
